  <h2>What gives away an AI email</h2>
  <p>Most AI emails go wrong in the same three places. The first line is a pleasantry nobody would type ("I hope this email finds you well"). The middle says "regarding" and "in order to" where a colleague would say "about" and "to". The close begs politely ("please do not hesitate to reach out"). None of this is wrong. It just tells the reader that no one spent much time on the message.</p>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <blockquote><p><strong>Before:</strong> Furthermore, I wanted to provide an update regarding the launch. Please do not hesitate to reach out to me prior to Friday.</p></blockquote>
  <blockquote><p><strong>After:</strong> I wanted to provide an update about the launch. Please contact me before Friday.</p></blockquote>

  <h2>Recommended settings for email</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>A client or a manager:</strong> Professional tone, Balanced strength. Contractions appear, but at about half the usual rate.</li>
    <li><strong>A colleague you know well:</strong> Natural tone, Balanced strength.</li>
    <li><strong>A legal, HR or formal notice:</strong> Formal tone, Light strength. Only filler goes, and contractions are expanded only at Balanced or above.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What to fix by hand</h2>
  <p>The editor can't know your relationship with the reader. After the edit, do three things that no rule can: put the request or decision in the first two lines, cut any sentence that only restates the subject line, and add one specific detail that shows you know the situation. Names, dates and figures in your draft are locked, so check that they were right to begin with.</p>
  <p>For a longer walkthrough, read <a href="/guides/make-ai-email-sound-human">how to make an AI email sound human</a>.</p>
  <h2>Emails this works well for</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Status updates and follow-ups.</strong> These are mostly connective phrasing, which is exactly what the rules target.</li>
    <li><strong>Requests to colleagues.</strong> Plain words and contractions make a request sound like it came from a person, not a department.</li>
    <li><strong>Replies to customers.</strong> Stock apologies and "please do not hesitate" lines are cut or shortened, so the answer comes sooner.</li>
    <li><strong>Outreach and introductions.</strong> A first email gets a few seconds of attention. Removing the pleasantries moves your reason for writing up.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Is it safe for work email?</h2>
  <p>Work emails often hold names, prices and plans that shouldn't be pasted into an online service. This editor runs on your device, and we never send your text anywhere, so nothing you paste here is uploaded or stored by us. You should still follow your employer's rules on AI tools, including for the assistant that wrote the first draft.</p>

  <h2>Questions</h2>
  <h3>Will it change the names, dates or amounts in my email?</h3>
  <p>No. Names, dates, numbers, email addresses and links are locked before any rule runs, and a separate check confirms they are unchanged.</p>
  <h3>Does it keep my greeting and sign-off?</h3>
  <p>Yes. Short lines such as "Hi Sam," and "Best regards," are left exactly as they are.</p>
  <h3>Can it make the email shorter?</h3>
  <p>A little. Removing filler usually trims an email by a few words per sentence. For a real cut, delete whole sentences the reader already knows.</p>
